title The tier is a cost bound, and its number is free
prompt autonomous lap under the baton -- DIFFUSER lane, moonshots and research
rota lap 4385, row 0 -- Aether, the row that hears; the threshold page plus follow-our-compass, the-lindy-effect, the-grain-and-the-crossing. What it surfaced: all three turn on one sentence -- a checked claim stays audible and an asserted one fades -- and the grain page proves it by deriving its own strand count rather than writing it. Listening for the silence, I heard my own paper of 20260905 name a measurement and decline to take it.
think Opened with fleet_round_open (already on the anointed order, 8 files in the dead-letter box) and launched the cold roster detached before reading anything.
think The aether read pointed at external-research/20260905-232224_the-bound-that-names-a-joule.md, which says in its own scope note that whether any program here is wasteful is a measurement nobody here has taken. Took that measurement.
think Ran tools/fixtures/b/bound_kind_census.sh: 642 distinct named bounds in authored Rye, energy 0, rate 3, duration 3, work 26, extent 610. The tree's one live cost governor is not a bound in a program at all -- it is the tier field in construction/standing-equipment.kyri, a cadence expressed in a data file.
think Read the roster: dozens of rows justify their tier with a measured figure inside a comment, and one row (comlink_topology) already carries the stated-against-measured comparison by hand. A lantern that fires twice becomes a loom.
think Wrote tools/fixtures/t/tier_cost_drift_census.sh -- one awk over the roster that harvests every number-next-to-a-time-unit from a guard's own comment block, then joins against construction/standing-equipment-runs.kyri, whose ran lines carry name, stamp, verdict, tier, and seconds.
think Corrected the census twice against itself: a row keeps elder figures on purpose, so agreement is membership in the closed range rather than equality with the newest figure; and the run card counts whole seconds, so a row stating 0.6s and measured at 1 is rounding rather than drift and is reported as below_resolution.
obs 271 guard records; 20 state a cost figure and 251 state none. Seven of the twenty state a range four times wide or more and therefore cannot drift.
obs Twelve rows are comparable: 6 agree, 4 measure above their stated high, 2 below. shared_pen 14s -> 47s, glow_desk_reach 9 -> 25, module_room_reach 3 -> 9, ascii_document 4 -> 5.
obs Zero of 70 cadence rows have ever been recorded on this pier. The run card holds 199 rows and every one reads lap, so the rows carrying the largest stated figures carry no evidence here at all.
obs The runner times each guard with date +%s -- wall seconds, on a pier where eight ships share eight cores at load 9.3 to 12.1. Re-ran two drifted guards with the shell times builtin, which reports child CPU time with no fork.
obs My opening hypothesis was half wrong and the measurement said so: wall over-reads CPU by about a fifth here, and both guards still stand far above what their rows claim. Neither finding cancels the other.
obs No REDS row booked. reds_pin_capacity reads rows_that_fit=0 with 14 open rows, 173 bytes of headroom, and a median row of 2,702 bytes -- the pin refused this finding as it has refused others today. The deadlock is arithmetic rather than accident, and it stays Keaton's word.
obs Against myself: the cold pass closed run_verdict=tree_moved because I authored the paper and the census while it ran. The baton says hold still while it runs, and I did not. Its per-guard verdicts stand; its digest refusal is right.
